        <h2 id="challenge-heading">The challenge</h2>

        <p class="w3-large">
            Faculty wanted every student to receive individualized feedback on
            their leadership experiences, strengths, performance conditions, and
            recurring behavioral patterns. Providing that level of personal
            analysis manually was not feasible at the scale of the course.
        </p>
        <p>
            The course already required students to complete multiple assessments,
            reflections, and phase-based assignments. The final assignment needed
            to use that work rather than ask students to start over. The design
            needed to help students combine their existing materials, identify
            connections across them, and turn those findings into a practical
            leadership playbook.
        </p>

        <p>
            The faculty member also wanted students to receive observations they
            might not generate independently. This included patterns across
            assignments, possible contradictions, repeated strengths, responses
            to pressure, and relationships among experiences that students had
            recorded at different points in the course.
        </p>

        <p>
            Generative AI could support this type of individualized pattern
            analysis, but its use introduced several design requirements:
        </p>

        <ul>
            <li>
                Students needed to retain control over what personal information
                they provided to the AI.
            </li>

            <li>
                The workflow needed to use an approved environment appropriate
                for course-related student data.
            </li>

            <li>
                Students needed to evaluate AI feedback instead of treating it as
                authoritative or automatically accurate.
            </li>

            <li>
                Instructions had to work for students with substantially different
                levels of AI experience.
            </li>

            <li>
                The assignment needed assessable criteria that did not grade
                students on private disclosures or on the quality of text generated
                by the AI.
            </li>
        </ul>

       
            
    </section>

    <!-- Solution -->
   <section
    id="solution"
    class="project-section w3-padding-32"
    aria-labelledby="solution-heading">
        <h2 id="solution-heading">The solution</h2>

       <p>
            I designed a multi-stage workflow that separates data collection,
            AI-supported analysis, student evaluation, and final synthesis.
        </p>

        <p>
            First, students assemble selected work from the course into a personal
            leadership database. This gives the AI a bounded source of
            student-provided information rather than relying on a single prompt or
            an unstructured conversation.
        </p>

        <p>
            Students then use a set of structured prompts to engage an AI coach in
            their individual Notebook. The prompts ask the AI to analyze the
            student-provided material, identify recurring patterns, surface
            potential connections, and ask follow-up questions. Students may
            continue the conversation for as long as it remains useful.
        </p>

        <p>
            The AI does not produce the final assignment. After each coaching
            conversation, students write their own evaluation of the response.
            They identify which observations were relevant, which were new or
            useful, and which were inaccurate, unsupported, or off-target.
        </p>

        <p>
            Students then use their prior work, the coaching conversation, and
            their evaluations of the AI's feedback to develop a final leadership
            playbook. This creates a traceable progression from course evidence to
            AI-assisted analysis to student-authored conclusions.
        </p>

        <p>
            The workflow provides individualized analysis without representing the
            AI as a substitute for faculty expertise. Faculty assess the student's
            interpretation, evidence, application of course concepts, and final
            synthesis rather than attempting to grade the AI-generated response.
        </p>

    </section>

